  subgraph "cluster_0" {
    label="The algebraic calculus rides on the toolchain classes for multiplication, division, and reciprocal. There is no toolchain class for exp , sin , or square root, so the one genuinely new artifact is a carrier capability — the function-layer analogue of the numeric carrier — and a family of smart constructors that each carry their own metrological discipline. The functions split into five families with genuinely different kind signatures: kind-preserving ( abs , min , max ); powers and roots (the dimension scales by the exponent); dimensionless transcendentals ( exp , log — pure number in, pure number out); the trigonometric functions (a plane angle in, a number out — curated so that sin of a reflectivity stays a category error); and logarithmic levels (dB, neper — where log of a ratio demotes the scale, so the product calculus correctly refuses it).";
